7231 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/P 367 0 R /F 4/M(D:20240624074238+08'00')/HighDpi false/QuadPoints[ 378.96 914.82 420.73 914.82 378.96 898.287 420.73 898.287]/Rect[ 378.96 898.287 420.73 914.82]/T(þÿÿÿ)/Subj(Highlight)/CreationDate(D:20240624073754+08'00')/C[ 0.717647 0.870588 0.909804]/CA 1/NM({403648ed-63dc-41dc-8e88-cd610bc376fb})/Popup 7232 0 R /AP<</N 7233 0 R >>/Contents(A little bit hard, First it cannot be a King move and it cannot be a pawn move. cause King cannot deliver a check and support the Queen on the second move. So it must be a Queen move, however, if White let Black's King escape to either d5 or f5, Black's King escaped. So this Queen move have to cover both squares, so will be the checkmate move, and the checkmate move is also quite symmetrical. So the answer is Qf3, followed by Qf7#.)>>
endobj
